            <ArticleTitle>Asthma incidence: data from the National Health Interview Survey, 1980-1996.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>65-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To obtain historical estimates of US asthma incidence from 17 years of health survey data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The 1980 through 1996 National Health Interview Survey contained a question asking about the time of asthma onset in persons with asthma. Annual past year incidence estimates were calculated from self-reports of asthma status.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Incidence increased from 2.5 per 1,000 (SE 0.37) in 1980 to 6.0 per 1,000 (SE 0.75) in 1996. Incidence increased faster in children than in adults and increased in females but not in males during this time.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These findings suggest that increasing asthma incidence contributed to the increasing prevalence during this time.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
